      "content": "I hear you, let’s give it a stern, no-nonsense approach:\n\n\"Imagine this: Picture a Mexican family, so they think about being 'late to dad's get-together', their car's the size of a clown car but packed to the brim with 'family business', and that's just the 'strictest' family member the Smiths know, always lobbying for 'lower taxes and higher stocks'. Fast forward, and they somehow swing from millennials entering college with scholarships pooled generously—because after all, we're rife with 'college recruitment drives'—to claiming they stopped all those online scams, sprinkle that community of 'concerned citizens' with assurance. As if all this juggling act doesn't raise eyebrows, let alone concern. So, there's this constant, uncanny oscillation: terrified that they might suddenly be the not-so-secret dealer in the neighborhood, yet simultaneously annoyed whenever they shun a Sunday football kick-off due to soccer season. It's the perfect embodiment of 'unexpected hazards with a playful side note'—except the side note is quite literal, and that's exactly how tradition molds the immigrant narrative into today's mixed 'dreams and nightmare.'\"",
